{"kind":"assert","text":"Survey of every worktree and branch in the gitseq checkout, on hugh's instruction of 2026-08-22, with the cleanup performed and two salvage findings that need owners.\n\nWHAT I MEASURED. Main at 7a02f3e1. I classified every worktree and branch by whether its tip is an ancestor of main, which is the only sound test for \"already in main\" -- a plain diff against main is symmetric and reports a branch that is merely behind as though it differed.\n\nCLEANUP DONE. Six worktrees whose head was an ancestor of main and whose checkout was clean are gone: the two temporary performance-compare trees under /private/var, gitseq-wt/base-check, gitseq-wt/guard-recut, and my own two review trees under the session scratchpad. 158 local branches wholly contained in main were deleted; the count fell from 220 to 62. Three refusals were correct -- request/citation-reanchor-fd0680ef, request/github-http-bounds and request/resident-memory-500k are checked out in worktrees, so git declined.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT TOUCH, DELIBERATELY. 74 of the 96 branches on origin are ancestors of main and could be pruned, but deleting a remote ref is outward-facing and I have not been told to publish that. Another session's scratchpad worktree under a3962402 is left alone; one writer per worktree. Every worktree holding an unmerged lane is left alone.\n\nFINDING ONE, WHICH IS THE REASON THIS ASSERT EXISTS. Two worktrees whose BRANCH is fully merged into main are carrying substantial uncommitted work that exists nowhere else. A tidy-up driven by branch state alone would have destroyed both, and that is exactly the mistake this survey was meant to avoid.\n\ngitseq-worktrees/github-http-bounds, branch at 33c29b1e, an ancestor of main. Uncommitted: 163 insertions across internal/connector/github/fetch.go and fetch_test.go. It adds a bound on the response body after decompression, a refusal to follow a redirect before resending the token, and page and total-item caps on issue listing, with four named tests including TestClientRefusesRedirectBeforeResendingToken and TestResponseBodyIsBoundedAfterDecompression. Those are security controls on an outward HTTP boundary, not scratch work.\n\ngitseq-worktrees/resident-memory-500k, branch at adda28c8, an ancestor of main. Uncommitted: 235 insertions across internal/kernel/checkpoint.go, internal/workroom/fold.go, internal/workroom/schema.go and their tests. It adds string interning in the fold with a pooledString JSON adapter that adopts unescaped bytes rather than copying, and a borrowed-chunk checkpoint path that compresses complete chunks from reader-owned memory and clones only the bounded tail. That is the memory work the 500k resident lane was about; its commitment was cancelled, so this is the only surviving copy.\n\nNeither is mine and I will not commit either. I am filing one request per finding so the decision has an owner, and both requests permit discard with a stated reason as a satisfying outcome -- the point is that somebody decides, not that the code lands.\n\nFINDING TWO, RECORDED WITHOUT A REQUEST. 36 unmerged branches have no worktree and no visible lane. All but four last moved between 2026-08-07 and 2026-08-12. Five carry real volume: task/deployment-story at 20 commits, request/commitment-honesty at 9 over 18 files, request/leased-activity-focus-current-main at 7 over 28 files, task/merge-artifact-paths at 6, request/docs-behavior-reanchor at 6 over 15 files. I am not proposing to delete or revive them: unlike the two above they are committed, so nothing is lost by leaving them, and deciding which abandoned designs to resurrect is a roadmap question rather than housekeeping.","body":{"branches_deleted":"158","main":"7a02f3e1d400ee12341979a9e54c3f82ec318e99","worktrees_removed":"6"}}